<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of 
<br class="ltx_break">LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shramay Palta      Peter Rankel
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sarah Wiegreffe       Rachel Rudinger</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Maryland, College Park, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{spalta,par,sarahwie,rudinger}@umd.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the degree to which human plausibility judgments of multiple-choice commonsense benchmark answers are subject to influence by (im)plausibility arguments for or against an answer, in particular, using rationales generated by <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s.
We collect <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3,000</annotation></semantics></math> plausibility judgments from humans and another <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13,600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13,600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13,600</annotation></semantics></math> judgments from <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s. Overall, we observe increases and decreases in mean human plausibility ratings in the presence of <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales, respectively, suggesting that, on the whole, human judges find these rationales convincing. Experiments with <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s reveal similar patterns of influence. Our findings demonstrate a novel use of <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s for studying aspects of human cognition, while also raising practical concerns that, even in domains where humans are “experts” (i.e., common sense), <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s have the potential to exert considerable influence on people’s beliefs.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        Our data and code will be released upon publication.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A typical framework for evaluating the commonsense reasoning abilities of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NLP</span> models is to present an everyday situation described in text (<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A person drops a glass</span>), followed by a set of possible inferences (<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The glass breaks</span>; <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The glass bounces</span>; etc), and instruction to select the most plausible answer choice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levesque et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roemmele et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mostafazadeh et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. Unlike in logical or mathematical reasoning problems, these answer choices are rarely definitively true or false, but rather lie on a continuum of plausibility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and require non-monotonic modes of reasoning to assess, such as defeasible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rudinger et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, abductive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhagavatula et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, or counterfactual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2020</a>)</cite> reasoning. In this setting, the answer designated as “correct” should be the choice that human judges agree is most plausible. Indeed, human annotators are more likely to agree on a “correct” answer when the plausibility gap between answer choices is large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, unless an answer choice is logically impossible, there exist circumstances under which even a relatively implausible distractor answer could be true. For example, one could argue that a glass is actually quite likely to bounce if dropped on a trampoline or rubber mat. By the same token, unless an answer choice is logically necessary, there exist circumstances under which an otherwise highly plausible outcome becomes much less likely: The drinking glass probably <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">won’t</span> break if it’s very sturdy or doesn’t fall far. Thus, any answer choice which is neither certain nor impossible can be subject to arguments for or against its plausibility. In principle, these types of (im)plausibility arguments or rationales do not introduce any new evidence to the situation; rather, they simply highlight possible circumstances which, <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if true</span>, would impact an answer’s plausibility.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08091v1/example.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/280;" width="476" height="280"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An example question and answer choice from <span id="S1.F1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor et al., 2019</a>)</cite> in <span id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFC20E;">yellow</span>, paired with <span id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated rationales in favor of the answer’s plausibility (<span id="S1.F1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S1.F1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">purple</span>); against the answer choice’s plausibility (<span id="S1.F1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, <span id="S1.F1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#ED1C24;">red</span>); and both for and against (<span id="S1.F1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>+<span id="S1.F1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, <span id="S1.F1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">green</span>). Likert scales on right show how mean plausibility ratings for the answer, as judged by human and <span id="S1.F1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> raters (OpenAI and Non-OpenAI), shift in response to different types of rationales.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate whether an <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> can produce convincing arguments for the plausibility or implausibility of commonsense benchmark answer choices, as measured by their ability to impact the plausibility ratings of human judges. (We will refer to such plausibility or implausibility arguments as <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> or <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales, respectively.) Posed another way, this paper asks whether and how human plausibility judgments change in response to LLM-written <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and/or <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To answer these questions, our work builds on the prior study of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>, who previously collected Likert-scale judgments from human annotators about the plausibility of individual question-answer choice pairs from two commonsense multiple choice benchmarks, Social IQA (<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span>; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sap et al. 2019</a></cite>) and CommonsenseQA (<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor et al. 2019</a></cite>). These judgments were collected in the absence of any rationales or (im)plausibility arguments, which we will refer to as the <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> rationale setting. We extend this work by sampling a subset of their annotated question-answer pairs, and using an <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> to generate <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales for or against the plausibility of each answer. Adopting a similar protocol, we then collect <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3,000</annotation></semantics></math> new human plausibility ratings of answer choices, presented alongside either a <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale of the answer, a <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationale, or both (<span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span>), as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In a parallel set of experiments using the same stimuli, we prompt <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> different <span id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s to also rate the plausibility of these answers, collecting an additional <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13,600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13,600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13,600</annotation></semantics></math> judgments from <span id="S1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s.</p>
</div>
