<td id="S1.T1.2.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">16,600(+1,000)</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Size and structure of the collected dataset. From left to right, columns report number of unique questions drawn from each dataset (#<math id="S1.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>); number of unique question-answer pairs (#<math id="S1.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math>); number of unique question-answer-rationale tuples (#<math id="S1.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a,r)</annotation></semantics></math>) over four settings (<span id="S1.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> rationale, <span id="S1.T1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale, <span id="S1.T1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationale, and <span id="S1.T1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationale); total number of human plausibility ratings collected; total number of <span id="S1.T1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> plausibility ratings generated; and total number of plausibility ratings (human or <span id="S1.T1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>). Numbers in parentheses represent <span id="S1.T1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">additional</span> judgments previously collected by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite> for the <span id="S1.T1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> rationale setting, used in our analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With this data, we are able to make the following observations:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both human and <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> plausibility judgments are significantly affected by the addition of <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO, CON</span>, and <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales, relative to a <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> rationale setting.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On average, <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales raise mean plausibility ratings from humans and <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> judges alike, while <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales lower mean ratings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These patterns hold across both gold and distractor answer choices, with one notable exception: mean plausibility ratings by humans surprisingly <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">drop</span> for gold answer choices when a <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale is presented. In <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s, mean plausibility ratings increase in response to <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales, for gold and distractor answer choices alike.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales yield mixed reactions from both humans and <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s, with ratings decreasing for the gold label, but increasing for the distractor answer choice.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models generally exhibit similar trends, we observe some differences. In particular, <span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales appear to have a larger effect on the OpenAI models’ ratings compared to the human ratings and Non-OpenAI models’ ratings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The initial plausibility rating of an answer choice has a strong anchoring effect: the higher the initial plausibility of an answer, the smaller the subsequent change in its rating after a rationale is provided.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Rationale Generation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduced a plausibility rating framework for rating the plausibility of individual answer choices from Multiple Choice Question (<span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MCQ</span>) datasets in isolation. For our study, we randomly sample a subset of 100 questions– 50 each from <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> and <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>– from the set of questions used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>. <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> is designed to evaluate the social and emotional intelligence in computational models. Each <span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> item consists of a short context describing a social situation, a question about a person in the situation and three answer choices. <span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>, on the other hand, evaluates general commonsense knowledge, with each item containing five answer choices. For each sampled question, we pick the dataset gold label (<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a randomly selected distractor answer choice (<math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math>) for our study. To maintain consistency with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we break down each question <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with answer choices <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math> into pairs <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in[a_{gold-label},a_{distractor}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in[a_{gold-label},a_{distractor}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then generate two forms of rationales for each <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">PRO<span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: An explanation that best explains the <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plausibility</span> of the answer choice for the given question and context (for <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CON<span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: An explanation that best explains the <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implausibility</span> of the answer choice for the given question and context (for <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These two rationales are then used to collect human and <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> plausibility judgments under four settings, described in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span>, <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, and <span id="S2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S2.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:57.3pt;vertical-align:-27.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-127.6pt,21.2pt) scale(0.574732289718158,0.574732289718158) ;">
<table id="S2.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent</span></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pro Rationale</span></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Con Rationale</span></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pro+Con Rationales</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Increase</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Decrease</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">No Change</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Increase</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Decrease</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">No Change</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Increase</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Decrease</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">No Change</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="S2.T2.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">SIQA</span></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Human</td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="69\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>69</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">69\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>29</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T2.2.1.3.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
