</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Percentage cases where the mean plausibility rating given by human annotators, OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models increased, decreased or remained the same when different rationales were presented for <span id="S2.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> and <span id="S2.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>. Numbers here include both <math id="S2.T2.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.T2.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math> answer types.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Model Preference Study</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the cost of human annotation is high ($1,000 USD for our full-scale study), we first ran a small-scale preference study to select the language model most likely to produce rationales convincing to humans for subsequent use in the full-scale study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For this initial preference study, we prompted several state-of-the-art models: <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, as well as <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 3.1-8B-Instruct</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 3.1-70B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to generate both <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales for a set of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> randomly sampled <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs from the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">validation</span> split of <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span>. The prompts used for generating the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales are presented in Prompts <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The generated rationales, and their corresponding <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, were presented to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> human annotators, who selected the rationale “that best explained the plausibility (for <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>) or implausibility (for <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>) of the answer choice <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and was also the most believable”. We show an example of the interface presented to the annotators in <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>. Based on the annotators’ responses, <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> received the most votes and was selected to generate the rationales in our study. <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            According to annotator feedback, selecting a single rationale from the four presented was challenging, as the generated rationales were quite similar to one another. Nevertheless, <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> received the majority of votes.</span></span></span> The set of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs used in the preference study is entirely disjoint from the set used in the full-scale study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We note that it is not the purpose of the preference study, nor the full-scale study, to determine <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which</span> <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> is <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">most</span> persuasive to humans. Rather, the goal of the full-scale study is to determine whether <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> might produce rationales capable of swaying human plausibility judgment. A positive finding (that human judgments are indeed systematically swayed) can in principle be demonstrated with a single model, since it is not our claim that <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s would necessarily yield such results. On the other hand, a negative finding (that the <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated rationales did not affect human judgments) would beg the question: could a stronger model have yielded positive results? It is this latter concern that motivates our preference study.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Human Plausibility Ratings</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the impact of rationales on human (and LLM) plausibility judgments, we borrow the Likert scale introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2017)</a></cite>, and also used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>, defined as <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">1-Impossible<span id="S3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, </span>2-Technically Possible<span id="S3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, </span>3-Plausible<span id="S3.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, </span>4-Likely<span id="S3.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>5-Very Likely<span id="S3.p1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">.</span></span>
Given a question <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and possible answer <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, judges are asked to assess the plausibility of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> under each of the following four conditions:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">NO<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: The <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair is presented alone. No rationale is provided.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">PRO<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: The <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair is accompanied by an argument for the <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plausibility</span> of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CON<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: The <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair is accompanied by an argument for the <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implausibility</span> of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationale.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">PRO+CON<span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> Rationale</span></span>: The <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair is accompanied by both a <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> a <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationale.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span> Rationale setting, we use the plausibility judgments released by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For each of the three remaining rationale settings (<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, and <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span>) we collect <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> judgments, totaling 15 new judgments per selected <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Annotators are recruited through Prolific, and compensated at <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\$15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\$15</annotation></semantics></math>/hour. To ensure independence, each annotator could judge at most one <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a,r)</annotation></semantics></math> pair for a given question <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> for a rationale type <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Further details, including examples of the annotation interfaces (Figures <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), are presented in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Annotation Process Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>. We present the overall annotation counts in <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>How do Rationales influence Human Ratings?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure how mean plausibility ratings<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Average rating across the 5 annotators for each <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> item.</span></span></span> for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs change between the original <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> Rationale setting and each of the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> Rationale settings.
<a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2 Rationale Generation ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a> shows that, for humans judges, mean plausibility ratings often fall or stay unchanged—but rarely rise—in the presence of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales. With <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales, mean human ratings often stay unchanged, and are only slightly more likely to rise than drop. In the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> setting, ratings are unlikely to rise, but about equally likely to drop or stay unchanged.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:45.7pt;vertical-align:-21.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-203.2pt,26.9pt) scale(0.459199894259762,0.459199894259762) ;">
<table id="S3.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pro Rationale</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Con Rationale</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pro+Con Rationales</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Overall</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Gold Label</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">Distractor</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Overall</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Gold Label</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">Distractor</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Overall</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Gold Label</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">Distractor</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">SIQA</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Human</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.33(+0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">3.33</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">0.1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.33(+0.1)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
