<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Non-OpenAI</td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.63(+0.61)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">3.63</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">0.61</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.63(+0.61)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.95(+0.31)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">3.95</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">0.31</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.95(+0.31)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.32(+0.92)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">3.32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">0.92</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.32(+0.92)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.18(-0.84)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">2.18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">0.84</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.18(-0.84)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.43(-1.21)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">2.43</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">1.21</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.43(-1.21)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.94(-0.47)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">1.94</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">0.47</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.94(-0.47)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m52" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" alttext="2.99(-0.03)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">2.99</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">0.03</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.99(-0.03)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m53" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" alttext="3.28(-0.36)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">3.28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB">0.36</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFDBDB;" mathbackground="#FFDBDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.28(-0.36)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T3.2.1.8.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><span id="S3.T3.2.1.8.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;"><math id="S3.T3.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.7(+0.3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">2.7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB">0.3</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBFFDB;" mathbackground="#DBFFDB" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7(+0.3)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Mean plausibility ratings given by Human, OpenAI, and Non-OpenAI agents after exposure to <span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, and <span id="S3.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales, reported separately for <math id="S3.T3.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T3.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math>. Numbers outside parentheses indicate mean ratings after rationales; numbers in parentheses represent the corresponding changes (<math id="S3.T3.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) from the No Rationale Plausibility Rating.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, an interesting pattern emerges when we stratify outcomes based on whether the answer choice is a “gold” (correct) or “distractor” (incorrect) answer, as shown in <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>.
Critically, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we observe that the effect of </span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> rationales on mean human judgments is bimodal</span>: on distractor answers, mean judgments rise, while for gold answers they fall. Thus, for implausible distractor answers, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales appear sufficiently persuasive to raise mean ratings. But why do ratings for gold answers drop? One possible explanation is that, for very likely answers, a mere <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plausibility</span> argument actually “undersells” the likelihood of the outcome; that is, people may interpret the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales as arguing the answer is <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">at most</span> plausible, rather than <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">at least</span> plausible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales, both gold and distractor answers show drops in mean plausibility, albeit with much larger drops for gold answers. (Interestingly, the drop for gold answers with <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales is over one full Likert scale point on average, the largest changes observed for any setting.) While boundary effects likely contribute to the differences observed between changes for gold and distractor labels (e.g., there is a cap on how much an already high rating can rise), they cannot account for differences between answers of the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">same</span> type (e.g. distractor) across different rationale settings. For example, we have seen that mean ratings for distractor answers rise with <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales and fall with <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales; in our controlled experimental setup, the only non-random explanation for this difference is the rationale type itself. (Significance tests are described at the end of this section.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales, we observe an approximate averaging of the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> outcomes: for gold and distractor answers both, changes in mean ratings lie in between the changes observed in the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> settings, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We perform several Chi-squared tests of homogeneity to compare the rating distributions with and without rationales. For <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span>, p-values for the shifts in the distributions are <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.069" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.069</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.069</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.17E^{-9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.17</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.17E^{-9}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.001</annotation></semantics></math> for the <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> settings, respectively; for <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>, they are <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.24E^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.24E^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0006</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0006</annotation></semantics></math>. In nearly all cases, adding rationales yields statistically significant distribution shifts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08091v1/siqa_all_agent_new.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Mean Plausibility Rating for <span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> for Different Agents for Different Rationale Conditions. <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows the mean changes for <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math> for all agents.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Do Rationales Affect LLMs as well?</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In an effort to understand whether <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s are also influenced by the introduction of different forms of rationales, we replicate the entire setup used to collect human plausibility ratings, as described in <a href="#S3" title="3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panickssery et al. (2024)</a></cite> has shown that <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s can recognize and favor their own generated outputs over outputs from other models. Given that <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> generated the <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales, to mitigate any potential biases that can arise from instances of self-preference, we divide the <span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s we study in our work into two groups, with membership as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAI Models</span>: GPT (3.5, 4, 4-turbo, 4o, 4o-mini, 4.5-preview, o1, and o3-mini) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024b</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-OpenAI Models</span>: LLaMA-3.1-Instruct (8B, 70B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, LLaMa-3.2-Instruct (3B), LLaMa-3.3-Instruct (70B), Mistral Instruct (8x22B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Yi-1.5-Chat (6B, 9B, 34B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek-R1 (Qwen-32B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We prompt the models listed above with the <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs accompanied by one rationale for each setting <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\texttt{NO,PRO,CON,PRO+CON}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">NO,PRO,CON,PRO+CON</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\texttt{NO,PRO,CON,PRO+CON}]</annotation></semantics></math> (same as used in human experiments), and present the zero-shot prompts in Prompts <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>, <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>, <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>LLM Ratings Pre and Post Rationales</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Paralleling our analysis in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we compare how mean plausibility ratings produced by LLMs (OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models) change in the presence of each rationale type, reporting results again in Tables <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2 Rationale Generation ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for direct comparison with results from the human judges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales have a larger positive impact for both sets of models, with a large proportion of ratings increasing across both datasets with the inclusion of the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationale. Similarly, adding <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales leads to a large drop in ratings across both datasets. The inclusion of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales once again elicits a mixed response, similar to our observation with human judgments in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, in sharp contrast to what we observed with human annotators, the introduction of <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales leads to an increase in the mean plausibility ratings for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> across both <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span> for both OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models. Similar to the shifts in human ratings for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math>, by adding <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales, both groups of models exhibit a decrease in mean plausibility ratings across both datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, when <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales are presented together with a <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> item, we observe the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s to also display mixed results, with plausibility ratings for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> dropping for both <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span>, but increasing for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math>. This behavior is similar to what was observed for the human ratings (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). One possible explanation for this phenomenon is that, if <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales dampen responses at the low end of the plausibility scale, and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales dampen responses at the high end of the plausibility scale, then <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales together may have the effect of pushing plausibility ratings to the center of the scale because neither extreme is being used for rating the answer <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. This would have the effect of raising average scores for low-scoring distractors <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{distractor})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{distractor})</annotation></semantics></math> and lowering scores for high-scoring gold-label answers <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{gold-label})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{gold-label})</annotation></semantics></math>, as is observed for both humans and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s.
Figures <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 LLM Ratings Pre and Post Rationales ‣ 4 Do Rationales Affect LLMs as well? ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that this is not simply a case of reversion to the mean, as, for instance, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales on average push mean scores of distractors (which are low to begin with) even lower.
Another compatible explanation is that, as observed in human judgments, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales result in shifts in mean judgments that lie between the shifts observed in the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To establish the significance of these shifts, we ran several Chi-squared tests of homogeneity to compare the ratings distributions with and without rationales within each setting. These shifts were found to be highly statistically significant across all settings, with all p-values <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08091v1/cqa_all_agent_new.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Mean Plausibility Rating for <span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span> for Different Agents for Different Rationale Conditions. <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.1 How do Rationales influence Human Ratings? ‣ 3 Human Plausibility Ratings ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows the mean changes for <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math> for all agents.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the ratings given by OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models, we see the OpenAI models to be more sensitive to the inclusion of <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales, which we hypothesize to be because of the OpenAI models favoring the rationales generated by <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We include examples from <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SIQA</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CQA</span> for each of <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales (<a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 6</span></a>), <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales (<a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 7</span></a>) and <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales (Tables <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.5 Prompts used for Rationale Generation and Plausibility Ratings ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), and show how human and <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> ratings differ when different rationales are included for a <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> item as compared to annotating without any rationales.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Why Did the Ratings Change?</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have now established that both humans and <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s are sensitive to the inclusion of <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> and <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales, and adjust their plausibility ratings accordingly. To better understand what factors contributed the most to the changes in the plausibility ratings, we perform ordinary least squares (<span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OLS</span>) regression on the collected data separately for <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{gold-label}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{gold-label}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{distractor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{distractor}</annotation></semantics></math> using the rationale type (<span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO,CON,PRO+CON</span>) and the <span id="S5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> Rationale Plausibility Rating<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Originally collected on the 1-5 Likert Scale as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For the regression, we use a <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-2,2]</annotation></semantics></math> scale to center the ratings around <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> as features, with the dependent variable being the change <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> in plausibility rating after a rationale is presented.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As detailed in <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5 Why Did the Ratings Change? ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, the <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> Rationale Plausibility Rating is a statistically significant predictor (<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) of the rating change for humans, OpenAI and Non-OpenAI models. The consistently negative coefficients reveal a strong anchoring effect: the higher the initial plausibility of an answer, the smaller the subsequent change in its rating after a rationale is provided. This suggests that all rater groups are less likely to raise their opinion if their initial assessment is already high. Additionally, this anchoring effect is more pronounced for distractors than for gold-label answers across all groups. This indicates that the initial rating of an incorrect answer choice has a much stronger influence on the final rating change than the initial rating about the correct one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among rationale types, <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> rationales exert a stronger effect than <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales, with large negative coefficients that sharply <span id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reduce</span> plausibility for otherwise correct answers. <span id="S5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span> rationales do <span id="S5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">increase</span> plausibility, but the magnitude of their coefficients is smaller, indicating that supporting reasons reinforce less than opposing reasons undermine. <span id="S5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span> rationales typically carry small negative coefficients, indicating that when both supporting and opposing arguments are presented together, the opposing component tends to dominate, yielding a net plausibility decrease.</p>
</div>
