<td id="S5.T4.2.1.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{1.1286}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1.1286</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{1.1286}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.2383" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2383</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.2383</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.8.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{0.1236}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0.1236</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{0.1236}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Non-OpenAI</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{0.5632}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0.5632</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{0.5632}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{0.6236}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0.6236</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{0.6236}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.9.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">0.6693</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">0.5428</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{0.8507}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0.8507</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{0.8507}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\textbf{0.8394}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0.8394</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\textbf{0.8394}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.0040" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0040</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.0040</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.9.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.0750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.0750</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>OLS Regression Coefficients for the <span id="S5.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NO</span> Rationale Plausibility Rating and Rationale Type (<span id="S5.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO</span>, <span id="S5.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CON</span> and <span id="S5.T4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRO+CON</span>) features. The dependent variable here is the change <math id="S5.T4.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> in the plausibility rating after a rationale was presented. Numbers highlighted in <span id="S5.T4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> represent coefficients that are statistically significant <math id="S5.T4.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p&lt;0.05)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p&lt;0.05)</annotation></semantics></math> according to the t-test.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we also test the rationale length, measured as the number of words in the generated rationale, as a feature in a separate regression where the dependent variable is the magnitude of the change in the plausibility rating, and present the results in <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.3 Computational Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Everything is Plausible: Investigating the Impact of LLM Rationales on Human Notions of Plausibility" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 5</span></a>. Across all settings, the regression coefficients are close to zero suggesting that rationale length exhibits only a weak linear relationship with rating scores, regardless of the answer choice type, dataset or the annotating agent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rating the likelihood or plausibility of events in language has been explored previously in commonsense reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The graded nature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, the subjectivity of commonsense reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Whiting and Watts, 2024</a>)</cite> along axes like culture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palta and Rudinger, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hershcovich et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatia and Shwartz, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Acquaye et al., 2024</a>)</cite> has been long studied. We take advantage of this subjectivity to introduce <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated rationales into a <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MCQ</span> and see the impact on human and <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> ratings. Previous works have also looked at biases in human probability estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kynn, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kruglanski and Ajzen, 1983</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martins, 2006</a>)</cite>, how framing effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Druckman, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mandel, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sher and McKenzie, 2008</a>)</cite> and recency bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones and Bergias, 1976</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steiner and Rain, 1989</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnold et al., 2000</a>)</cite> impact human decision making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s can be trained or prompted to produce rationales of many forms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiegreffe and Marasovic, 2021</a>)</cite>; early work on training <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LMs</span> to produce free-form textual rationales focused on explaining model predictions on computer vision or natural language inference tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendricks et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Camburu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LMs</span>’ ability to generate free-form textual explanations for commonsense reasoning tasks was first established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajani et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shwartz et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Latcinnik and Berant (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brahman et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiegreffe et al. (2021)</a></cite>, with subsequent work showing that large pre-trained <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LMs</span> could generate high-quality explanations for commonsense tasks without having been explicitly trained to do so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paranjape et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lampinen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiegreffe et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marasovic et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is now widely accepted that <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LMs</span> can produce fluent textual explanations for a wide variety of NLP tasks. The role of these explanations on human (over-)trust, reliance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and utility to humans doing tasks has been studied in a variety of task settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biran and McKeown, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng and Boyd-Graber, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">González et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fok and Weld, 2024</a>)</cite>, though without a specific focus on understanding how explanations affect humans’ perception of the plausibility of commonsense scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, by collecting <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3000</annotation></semantics></math> human and <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13,600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13,600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13,600</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> ratings for <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, we are able to demonstrate that both human and <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> plausibility ratings shift when rationales either in favor, against, or presented together are included with a commonsense reasoning question <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and answer <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the potentially persuasive nature of <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated explanations, specifically for commonsense reasoning tasks. Our findings also highlight that humans and <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s do not always react similarly when shown different rationales, with models showing a higher sensitivity to the rationales, underscoring the challenges of aligning AI-generated judgments with human intuition, particularly in subjective reasoning tasks. Finally, our findings highlight the potential for <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s to aid humans by challenging users’ reasoning, stress-testing arguments, and introducing alternative perspectives, all potentially leading to more effective human-AI collaboration. However, this persuasive nature of <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>s could make them particularly effective tools for shaping opinions, potentially undermining autonomy and informed decision-making. Addressing these risks requires robust safeguards, including transparency, bias mitigation, and mechanisms to detect and counteract harmful manipulative uses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal in this work was to determine whether <span id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> <span id="Sx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> might produce rationales capable of swaying human (and other LLMs’) plausibility judgments. For that purpose, <span id="Sx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> proved effective at creating convincing arguments which, we demonstrated, led to the changes in plausibility ratings. However, the persuasive power and style of <span id="Sx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated rationales can vary significantly across different models and architectures. We hope that our findings inspire future works to look into the extent to which different models influence human and <span id="Sx1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span> plausibility judgments.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The scope of our investigation is confined to two English-language commonsense reasoning datasets, Social IQA and CommonsenseQA. The plausibility of an answer choice and the extent to which a rationale is persuasive might differ in other languages and domains, such as technical, scientific, or legal reasoning, where arguments may be more constrained by factual evidence. Future work may explore understanding the impact of <span id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM</span>-generated rationales on domains beyond commonsense reasoning. Furthermore, our human annotators were recruited from a specific demographic (US-based, English-speaking users on Prolific). As common sense itself can be culturally situated, the impact of these rationales could vary across different cultural and linguistic groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While we employed rigorous annotation protocols and made sure each instance was annotated by at least <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> annotators, there may still be instances where ambiguity in interpretation arises and hence, the impact a rationale has on the plausibility ratings would be different.</p>
</div>
</section>
